 "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: the series prints, in the editors' own chapters on recognition and revolution, the tests the United States applied to Latin American governments that took power by force from 1895 to 1980, and the demands it made before recognizing them: Wilson's insistence on a constitutionally elected government and on an election in which the usurper did not stand (1913–1921); the Central American rule of Article II of the 1923 treaty, applied until 1936; Stimson's three questions of 1930, control without active resistance, fulfilment of international obligations and elections in due course; wartime consultation and the test of friendliness to the Allied cause (1943–1947); the Bogotá rule of 1948 that continuing relations implies no approval, with three assurances asked of each junta; Kennedy's return to conditions in 1962–1963; and the drift after 1965 toward recognizing countries rather than governments. Where FRUS points for the rest: the documents came overwhelmingly from the Department of State's Central Decimal File, 1910–1949 (Record Group 59; the internal-affairs classes of each country), and after 1963 from the Central Files 1964–1969, the Kennedy, Johnson and Carter national security files, and Nixon's NSC files; the footnotes cite without printing the Department's lot files for Central America (lot 63D127, lot 64D16), the Secretary's memoranda of conversation (lot 65D330), NSC policy papers (lot 66D95) and the Muskie and Vance briefing books (lots 82D100, 84D241), all now Record Group 59 series at College Park, several still restricted. What this request cannot get from FRUS: the 1950s cases (Cuba 1952, Bolivia 1952, Guatemala 1954, Argentina 1955), Brazil 1964 and Argentina 1976 are not printed under recognition headings and were not read here; the texts of the Estrada doctrine and of the inter-American resolutions of 1943 and 1965 are cited but not printed; and the Latin American governments' own reasoning survives on